# tlo: zaduszki
On All Souls’ Day the whole of Zapadlina went to Bukowiec. There was a smell of smoke and frozen earth, and fallen leaves crunched underfoot. In the churchyard hundreds of tallow candles were burning, and among the graves people walked with little lamps, whispering prayers for those who had gone. After dark they made their way home in silence, in single file, leaving behind them a sea of trembling flames.
At home Mother laid the table as for a feast day, with one bowl too many. On the windowsill she left a slice of bread and a cup of water – for the forefathers, as she said. # pokaz: parapet
{ figurka_u_staruchy:
    Father set a second bowl beside it. He did not say who it was for, but Jaromir knew: for Maciek and Marianna.
- else:
    “For your grandfather,” his father muttered, topping up the cup with water. “He always liked his cup full.”
}
In the night something woke Jaromir. Moonlight fell through a crack in the shutter and lay across the windowsill. The cup of water stood untouched. But the slice of bread was gone – only crumbs were left. # pokaz: okruchy
From the direction of the forest, far off, someone called out long and drawn, and from the other end of the valley, from the marshes, something answered.
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
* [Carry a lighted lamp to the wayside shrine at the forest’s edge – for those who wander in the dark.]
    ~ chleb_dla_dziadow = true
    # tlo: kapliczki_noc
    He took the little lamp down from the shelf, slipped out of the cottage and set it beneath the shrine at the edge of the woods – just as the old folk used to do long ago, so that the souls from under the forest too might find their way to their own. When he turned round, the branches rustled, and the small flame lay down flat, pointing towards the village. # pokaz: jaromir
    At dawn he went back to the shrine. The lamp stood empty, licked clean of its tallow, and on the shrine’s little roof lay a fresh green oak leaf – in November, when every oak was long since bare. His father was waiting for him on the threshold. He did not ask where he had been – he only looked at what his son held in his hand and seized him by the scruff of the neck, for the first time in years.
    “On All Souls’ nobody goes near the forest,” he said quietly. “There’s no knowing who’ll follow him back to the cottage.” # dalej
