# tlo: zima_przedzenie
~ zapiski += uboze_2
In the middle of November snow fell and for a long time would not melt. The road over the ridge was buried knee-deep, the lake froze so hard that the boys slid across it from shore to shore, and Urbaniak’s sawmill stood still – the great wheel was furred with ice, and for three months the village lay in a hush such as Jaromir had not known since he was small.
The evenings were spent indoors. The women spun flax at their spinning wheels until the whirring filled the room, and in February they gathered for feather-stripping – several neighbours to one table, over a basket of goose down that flew into every corner whenever the door was opened. Over that work stories were told – of strzygas, of planetniks, of the maiden who danced with a stranger till dawn – and the girls squealed with fright and laughed by turns. Hanka came with her mother and always sat where she could see Jaromir across the table. # pokaz: pierze
{ uboze:
- nakarmione:
    That winter Mother marvelled that not once did mice get into the larder, and that there was always more milk in the jug than had been poured into it. Jaromir knew whom to thank for that, and every evening he set a thimble full of cream behind the stove. # pokaz: uboze
    At Staś’s, on the other hand, the milk soured in the pots all winter long, and old Staś’s wife swore that someone was skimming off her cream at night. Jaromir did not tell her who.
- przegnane:
    That winter was a hard one for them. The hens stopped laying, something in the larder was forever spoiling, and Mother complained that the cottage seemed not to like them.
}
Franek hardly left the house until spring. Antek said that his brother sat all day long at the window that faced the lake, staring at the ice as if waiting for it to thaw. “At night too,” he would add, more quietly. “I wake up, and he’s still there.” # dalej
