= zielone_swiatki
# tlo: swiatki
At Whitsun the whole of Zapadlina smelled of sweet flag – the women carried it up from the lake by the armful and strewed the long leaves over the earthen floors, the men fastened young birch boughs to the corners of the cottages and drove them into the ground by the thresholds, and the children decked the wayside shrines with garlands of marsh marigolds until the posts vanished beneath the yellow flowers.
Over the winter Jaromir had grown so much that his mother had to sew him new shirts and breeches – the old ones had become too small and hampered his movements. His voice cracked at the worst possible moments – especially when Hanka was near.
She had changed too. Her plait was longer now than ever, and when she walked through the village the boys from Bukowiec turned to stare after her, sometimes tripping over their own feet.
Towards evening she came to the fence with a bunch of freshly picked herbs. # pokaz: hanka
“Grandmother sent it,” she said, pressing into his hand a bundle of grey-green stalks with a bitter, sharp smell. “Wormwood. At Whitsun the creatures come up out of the water and dance, and they fear wormwood more than fire. So Grandmother says. But she also says whistling at table brings want, so you choose for yourself what to believe.”
She blushed, as if she had said something foolish, and lowered her eyes.
