# tlo: rusalki
Jaromir ran after him. Hanka ran right beside him, her skirt hitched up, and behind them came Antek – stumbling in the dark and calling his brother in a voice that rose higher and higher and kept breaking. They forced their way through the reeds, sinking ankle-deep in the mud, and stopped.
In the shallows, in the silver light of the moon, three girls were dancing. # pokaz: rusalki
They were young and very pale, in linen shifts that clung to their bodies, wet and heavy with water. Long, loose hair reached to their waists, threaded with waterweed and the white flowers of water lilies, and on their heads they wore wreaths of rushes. One was whirling round and round, splashing water. The second was holding out her hand straight towards Franek – at her throat glowed red rowan-berry beads. The third was pushing wet strands back from her brow and looking at Jaromir.
Their faces were pretty – too pretty – and they smiled a little too widely. And their eyes… their eyes were wholly black, with no whites. Water dripped from their fingers and their hair, drop after drop, without end.
Franek was already waist-deep in the lake. The rusalka who had reached out to him took his hand and drew him – gently, tenderly – towards the deep.
“Franek!” Hanka screamed.
He did not even look round.
Antek flung himself after his brother. After three steps the bottom dropped away beneath his feet – he had never learned to swim – and the boy went under with his mouth open. Hanka leapt in after him, caught him by the collar and dragged him back into the shallows. He coughed, spitting water, and fought her with all his might.
“Let go! He’s my brother! Let me go!”
“I won’t,” she panted, and pinned him to the mud with her own weight. “Jaromir, save him!”
