    Then Hanka cried out – not to him, but to the one with the rowan-berry beads.
    “Kaśka! Wójcik’s Kaśka! Let him go! If you ever loved him, let him go!”
    Only now did Jaromir recognise her. All one summer that girl had followed Franek about like a shadow, and he had laughed at her in front of his friends and danced with other girls at the harvest festival. On Midsummer Night, three springs ago, she had gone down to the lake and never come back. They searched for her for a week, but the water never gave her back.
    The rusalka went still. For a single instant something human flickered across her black eyes – grief, perhaps shame – and her fingers loosened their grip. That was enough. Jaromir dragged his friend into the shallows and both of them collapsed in the mud, gasping. Behind him he could still hear someone weeping softly over the water. # pokaz: znikaja # dalej
