    “That was Kaśka,” Hanka whispered, when she had found her voice again. “The one with the beads. Kaśka, the Wójciks’ daughter.”
    All one summer Kaśka had followed Franek about like a shadow, and he had laughed at her in front of his friends and danced with other girls at the harvest festival. On Midsummer Night, three springs ago, she had gone down to the lake and never come back. They searched for her for a week, but the water never gave her back.
