Franek went pale and turned his back on them.
“I knew,” he said dully. “I’ve been dreaming of her for a year. Wet, in those beads. She kept asking me to come and dance.”
By morning the whole village had heard all about it. The old folk nodded their heads and repeated a single word: rusalki. Some said they came out of the water until St John’s Day, others until the harvest, and old Staś’s wife insisted it was all year round, only in summer you could see them. On one thing they agreed: after dark no one with any sense whistles by the water.
